    "narrative": "For LawDog\u2019s roster on August 29th, **Bryce Harper** (78.57) and **Lawrence Butler** (74.61) headline the lineup as our primary offensive drivers. **Bryce Harper** draws a highly favorable matchup against Ryan Johnson (5.16 xERA), and his elite xwOBA metrics combined with a premium #3 spot make him a must-start staple. Similarly, **Lawrence Butler** benefits from a massive platoon advantage against Shane Baz and a favorable ballpark factor (+10%), pushing his efficiency score well into the elite tier. **Jos\u00e9 Ram\u00edrez** (72.52) remains a rock-solid play despite the matchup against Daniel Lynch IV, as his consistency and high-leverage spot in the batting order insulate his production from volatility.\n\nStrategic platoon edges and favorable park conditions have elevated several secondary options, most notably **Luis Arraez** (69.53) and **Jonathan Aranda** (63.13). **Luis Arraez** is hitting the sweet spot of a weak opposing starter and a beneficial lineup position, while **Jonathan Aranda** gains significant value (+12%) from his platoon advantage against Walker Buehler. The closest call on the bench was **Dylan Beavers**, whose 54.01 score was bolstered by park factors, yet he ultimately lost out to the superior platoon-adjusted projections of **Braden Montgomery** and **Gavin Sheets**. \n\nRisk management remains a priority given that nearly all matchups currently rely on projected batting orders. While **Justin Crawford** (63.67) is an intriguing play for his stolen base potential, his placement in the #9 hole acts as a drag on his overall scoring efficiency. Users should monitor the official lineup cards closely; any move for **Cal Raleigh**, **Yainer Diaz**, **Gleyber Torres**, or **Zack Gelof** out of their projected spots could necessitate a re-evaluation of the daily efficiency baseline, particularly if high-floor bench assets like **Daulton Varsho** or **Jung Hoo Lee** find themselves in better lineup spots than initially anticipated.",
